From u16f43467e4ee3f24 at groups.google.com  Tue Feb  9 16:35:15 2021
From: u16f43467e4ee3f24 at groups.google.com (Mei-Ling Bai)
Date: Tue, 09 Feb 2021 16:35:15 +0000
Subject: Proper explanation for using "distance to transect" in line transect surveys
Message-ID: <w86L4l1Zjng.N8nsY0qSBAAJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/distance-sampling/c/w86L4l1Zjng/m/N8nsY0qSBAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi All,

I am working on line transect bird survey data with a colleague who is experienced in applying distance sampling with point count surveys. It's hard for him to accept using "distance to transect" for modelling instead of using "distance to observer". He argues that, for a bird detected 200 m ahead (the transect direction), the distance to transect might just be 5 m. That would lead to an underestimate of detection distances and thus the ESW. He also argues that, a transect is just an ever-moving point count, so the method should not be different. What would be the proper way to explain the line-transect distance sampling to a person imprinted by point-count distance sampling?

Any suggestion is appreciated!

Mei-Ling

From uf2122b34f1aa6e95 at groups.google.com  Tue Feb  9 16:52:54 2021
From: uf2122b34f1aa6e95 at groups.google.com (Eric Rexstad)
Date: Tue, 09 Feb 2021 16:52:54 +0000
Subject: Re: {Suspected Spam} [distance-sampling] Proper explanation for using "distance to transect" in line transect surveys
Message-ID: <w86L4l1Zjng.nwL0_UCTBAAJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <w86L4l1Zjng.N8nsY0qSBAAJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
References: <w86L4l1Zjng.N8nsY0qSBAAJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/distance-sampling/c/w86L4l1Zjng/m/nwL0_UCTBAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Mei-Ling

This is not a extended discussion of the challenges of using distance to observer in line transect analysis.  Instead, I pass along a reference to a paper we wrote a number of years ago on this subject in response to the common use of animal-to-observer analysis in the primate literature
Buckland, S. T., Plumptre, A. J., Thomas, L., & Rexstad, E. A. (2010). Line transect sampling of primates: Can animal-to-observer distance methods work? International Journal of Primatology, 31(3), 485–499. https://doi.org/10.1007/s10764-010-9408-4

