Hi Rasmus,

I wonder if this might be a problem with regional differences in decimal notation (dots vs commas etc)?  This should not be a problem in Distance for Windows but has been on occasion, historically.  You could try setting your region to UK and see if that fixes it.

Another option, if you're doing some more "advanced" work with the dsm engine, would be to switch to working entirely in R.  This gives considerably more flexibility with your analysis and output options.
Running analyses in debug mode allows to access the R input files as one way of learning the ropes -- there are also the workshop materials from Dave Miller's last DSM workshop freely available at

https://eur01.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fworkshops.distancesampling.org%2Fonline-dsm-2020%2F&amp;data=04%7C01%7C%7Cef257da443be4d500cd408d8d74b88f3%7C89f0873991c047aea732291b5df7a94e%7C0%7C0%7C637496066711860640%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJWIjoiMC4wLjAwMDAiLCJQIjoiV2luMzIiLCJBTiI6Ik1haWwiLCJXVCI6Mn0%3D%7C1000&amp;sdata=EjBC55cEl6dKABHZ7pe7%2BhhWvSAaVdLoQ3jHW2B9N8A%3D&amp;reserved=0

(although likely not as good as getting it directly from Dave :) ).

As a halfway house, you can run analyses in R using the debug mode outputs to see what's being generated by R.  For example, I opened the "Dolphins" example from the Sample Projects that come with R, and ran the analyses in there up to analysis number 6, which does the predictions.  I then ran that one in debug mode, and found the file it creates "in.r" in a temporary directory.  The line that generate the prediction grid is

dsm.predict.6<- predict(dsm.5,newdata=grid.6, off.set=grid.6$area)

and then the one that writes this to file is (in my case - your directory will differ)

sink(file='C:\\Users\\len\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\dst43677\\res.r',append=T)

so I could have a look at that file and see what's in it.  I could also just have a look at dsm.predict.6 in R.

I hope this is helpful - just ping on me off the list if you want to discuss more and we can report back to the list once resolved.

Cheers, Len

